  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 154 PM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nMostly clear skies are expected through Friday as high pressure\ntracks through the western Great Lakes region.\n\nStorm Chances Return Late Friday: Next chance for \nshowers/thunderstorms comes late Friday night into Saturday \nmorning as an upper trough drags a cold front across the region. \nThe risk for any severe storms is very low (less than 5%) as \nforecast soundings show a stout capping inversion during the \nperiod. Guidance also continues to the axis of greatest \ninstability and moisture remaining well west of the forecast area.\nThere is still some timing uncertainty with when rain may come to\nan end Saturday as recent model guidance has come in slower with \nthe passage of the front which could lead to showers lingering \nthrough the morning Saturday. Overall the trend with the\nrain from this front has diminished from previous runs as some\nmodels suggest the front could pass through with little to no rain\nas the best dynamics stay to the north with the best instability\nto the south. This is reflected in the latest NBM pops with a\nsmall chance (<10%) across east-central Wisconsin and the\nlakeshore. Weak high pressure is then progged to move in \nreturning dry conditions for the remainder of the weekend.\n\nHeavy Rain and Storms Early Next Week: A dynamic synoptic system\nis progged to lift northeast across the northern Plains and\nwestern Great Lakes Monday and Tuesday. Global ensembles show a\nstrong mass response ahead of this system with PWATS increasing\nnear 2\" which is around 200% of normal for this time of year. The\nhigh moisture content and strong dynamical lift does support an\nincreased risk for heavy rainfall with NBM probs showing 30-60% \nchances greater than 2\" of rain across much of the forecast area,\nwith the highest chances in central Wisconsin. There is also a \nlow risk for a few stronger storms to develop Monday as strong \n~50kt deep layer shear overspreads an unstable airmass; however, \nforecast soundings show a stout inversion near the surface which \nmay keep storms elevated rather than surface based. \n\nRest of the forecast: There continues to be low chances (20-30%) \nfor precipitation during the middle of next week as zonal flow\nsets up across the Great Lakes. This period could just as easily\nbe dry as it could be light showers as the models are uncertain\nhow close a surface boundary will be to the region. If the high\nwins out we stay dry, if the boundary is further north we could\nsee some showers at times. The one thing that seems certain is\nthat we will be noticeably cooler with highs only in the 60s.",
